<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julius Caesar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">100–<a href="/years/44-bce">44 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julius Caesar was a Roman general and statesman whose victory in civil war made him dictator, before his assassination in 44 BCE. His intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a>, and their relationship drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a> into Roman politics. His career paved the way for the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> under Augustus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" width="309" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his victory over the Pompeians and Juba I at the Battle of Thapsus in 46 BCE, <b>Julius Caesar</b> annexed much of Numidia as a second province, Africa Nova, so that the old province became Africa Vetus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pharnaces II kept the Bosporus and later tried to recover his father’s kingdom, but <b>Julius Caesar</b> defeated him at Zela in 47 BCE, the victory he summed up as ‘veni, vidi, vici’.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Their lands formed part of the Republican province of Hispania Ulterior, and campaigns by Decimus Junius Brutus in 138–137 BCE, by <b>Julius Caesar</b> as governor in 61–60 BCE and by others completed the conquest, interrupted by the war of Sertorius in the 70s BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hiempsal's son Juba I backed Pompey and the Senate against <b>Julius Caesar</b>, and after the death of Pompey he joined the republican leaders in Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The reign of Cleopatra VII (r. 51–30 BCE) represented the final phase of the dynasty, during which she sought to preserve Egyptian independence through alliances with <b>Julius Caesar</b> and Mark Antony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Julius Caesar</b> crossed twice, in 55 and 54 BCE, defeating Cassivellaunus and taking hostages and promises of tribute, but he left no garrison.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Theatre of Pompey, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-64" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Julius Caesar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-64">What was here in 64 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;All Gaul is divided into three parts, one of which the Belgae inhabit, the Aquitani another, those who in their own language are called Celts, in ours Gauls, the third.&#8221;</p><footer>The Gallic War, book 1 &#183; tr. W. A. McDevitte and W. S. Bohn, 1848 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10657/pg10657-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…in most cases men willingly believe what they wish.&#8221;</p><footer>The Gallic War, book 3 &#183; tr. W. A. McDevitte and W. S. Bohn, 1848 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10657/pg10657-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let us go whither the omens of the Gods and the iniquity of our enemies call us. The die is now cast.&#8221;</p><footer>Suetonius, Life of Julius Caesar, 32 &#183; tr. Alexander Thomson, 1796, rev. T. Forester &#183; Suetonius' account of the crossing of the Rubicon &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6400/pg6400-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For my part, I had rather be the first man among these fellows, than the second man in Rome.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Caesar &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; passing a poor village in the Alps, when his companions joked about its politics &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have not just cause to weep, when I consider that Alexander at my age had conquered so many nations, and I have all this time done nothing that is memorable?&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Caesar &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to his friends in Spain, who found him in tears after reading the history of Alexander &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
